INT. HOUSING COURT - COURTROOM 3 - MORNING

A cramped courtroom with a leaking ceiling tile and a plastic bucket underneath. A worn carpet, three rows of benches, a flag on a crooked pole.

JUDGE HORTENSE ABBOTT-LUND, 60s, reading glasses perched on a long nose, peers over the bench. LANDLORD DEXTER OLUYEMI, 40s, cufflinks, signet ring, stands at one table. TENANT MARISELA QUINTANILLA, 30s, in her work uniform from a bakery, flour still on one sleeve, stands at the other. Beside her, ATTY. CORNELIUS PRATT, 50s, a threadbare suit, a briefcase with a broken clasp.

ABBOTT-LUND
Mr. Oluyemi, you've served notice?

DEXTER
Yes, Your Honor.

ABBOTT-LUND
Mr. Pratt, you dispute it?

PRATT
We do, Your Honor. My client wasn't informed of the rent increase.

DEXTER
It was posted in the lobby.

MARISELA
There's no lobby. There's a hallway.

DEXTER
Same thing.

MARISELA
There's a hallway with a broken light. I can't read in the dark.

ABBOTT-LUND
Ms. Quintanilla, please address me.

MARISELA
Sorry, Judge.

ABBOTT-LUND
Mr. Pratt, any further objection?

PRATT
Only that Mr. Oluyemi has been collecting rent for a unit that doesn't have a working stove for six months.

DEXTER
I replaced the stove.

MARISELA
With a hot plate.

ABBOTT-LUND
That's enough.

The bucket under the ceiling tile pings with a single drop.
